  <h1>Quantum Bharat: India's National Quantum Mission</h1>

  <section class="explain-simple">
    <div class="kicker">In simple words</div>
    <p>
      In April 2023, India placed its biggest-ever bet on quantum technology. The Union Cabinet approved
      the <strong>National Quantum Mission (NQM)</strong> — an eight-year, <strong>₹6,003.65
      crore</strong> programme to take India from quantum research papers to quantum hardware: working
      quantum computers, hack-proof quantum communication, ultra-precise quantum sensors, and the
      materials to build them all.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Who runs it?</strong> The <strong>Department of Science &amp; Technology (DST)</strong> is
      the nodal agency, and the mission is chaired by <strong>Ajai Chowdhry</strong>. The money is
      front-loaded toward the middle years of the mission — the peak funding year is
      <strong>2025-26 (₹1,261 crore)</strong>, with 2026-27 close behind at ₹1,113.7 crore.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>How is it organised?</strong> Around four technology verticals — <strong>quantum
      computing</strong>, <strong>quantum communication</strong>, <strong>quantum sensing &amp;
      metrology</strong>, and <strong>quantum materials &amp; devices</strong> — each with staged
      targets. Research is anchored in <strong>four Thematic Hubs (T-Hubs)</strong> set up in 2024-25 at
      IISc Bengaluru, IIT Madras (with C-DOT), IIT Bombay and IIT Delhi, together spanning
      <strong>43 institutions and 152 researchers</strong> across 17 states and 2 Union Territories.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Where does it stand?</strong> The hubs are established and running, fabrication facilities
      are coming up, eight startups were selected under the mission in November 2024, and headline
      demonstrations — a 25-qubit Indian quantum computer, 100-km-plus quantum key distribution — have
      begun. But spending has lagged ambition: in 2024-25 only about <strong>₹32.77 crore of the ₹86
      crore allocated</strong> was actually utilized. This page keeps announced targets and delivered
      outcomes clearly separate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <details class="deeper">
    <summary>Go deeper — the four verticals, the money, and the hubs</summary>
    <div class="deeper-body">
      <h3>The four verticals and their official targets</h3>
      <p>
        The mission's detailed objectives spell out staged, measurable goals. <strong>Quantum
        computing</strong> aims for intermediate-scale machines with 20–50 physical qubits within 3
        years, 50–100 within 5 years, and <strong>50–1,000 physical qubits within 8 years</strong>, on
        superconducting and photonic platforms. <strong>Quantum communication</strong> targets
        satellite-based secure communication between two ground stations over <strong>2,000 km</strong>
        within India (and with other countries), inter-city quantum key distribution over
        <strong>2,000 km</strong> on existing optical fiber, and multi-node (2–3 node) quantum networks
        with quantum memories. <strong>Quantum sensing &amp; metrology</strong> aims for magnetometers
        sensitive to 1 femto-Tesla, gravity sensors accurate to 100 nm/s², and atomic clocks stable to
        one part in 10¹⁹. <strong>Quantum materials &amp; devices</strong> covers superconductors, novel
        semiconductors, topological materials, and single and entangled photon sources and detectors.
        These are approved targets — the finish line, not the current position.
      </p>
      <h3>The money, year by year</h3>
      <p>
        The approved year-wise outlay ramps up through the mid-mission years: ₹704.1 crore (2023-24),
        ₹1,178.6 crore (2024-25), <strong>₹1,261.0 crore (2025-26)</strong>, ₹1,113.7 crore (2026-27),
        then tapering to ₹804.3, ₹556.9, ₹212.1 and ₹172.9 crore. But allocation is not spending. In FY
        2024-25, ₹86 crore was allocated, ₹43.07 crore was disbursed, and only <strong>₹32.77
        crore</strong> was utilized — about 76% of what was disbursed, and barely a third of what was
        allocated. Analysts have flagged absorptive capacity — the system's ability to actually deploy
        the money into labs, equipment and people — as the mission's quietest risk.
      </p>
      <h3>The four Thematic Hubs</h3>
      <p>
        Established in FY 2024-25 and incorporated as Section-8 (not-for-profit) companies, each with
        its own governing board, the hubs are the mission's research backbone:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Quantum Computing</strong> — IISc Bengaluru ("Foundation for QC Innovation").</li>
        <li><strong>Quantum Communication</strong> — IIT Madras, in association with C-DOT, New Delhi ("IITM CDOT Samgnya Technologies Foundation").</li>
        <li><strong>Quantum Sensing &amp; Metrology</strong> — IIT Bombay ("Qmet Tech Foundation").</li>
        <li><strong>Quantum Materials &amp; Devices</strong> — IIT Delhi ("QMD Foundation").</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Together they comprise 14 Technical Groups and 17 Project Teams. Central fabrication and
        research facilities are being set up at IISc Bengaluru, IIT Bombay, <strong>IIT Kanpur</strong>
        and IIT Delhi — the shared workshops where India's quantum hardware will actually get built.
      </p>
      <h3>Startups under the mission</h3>
      <p>
        In November 2024, the Minister of State for Science &amp; Technology announced <strong>eight
        startups selected under NQM guidelines</strong> — QNu Labs, QpiAI, Dimira Technologies,
        Prenishq, QuPrayog, Pristine Diamonds, Quanastra and Quan2D — spanning quantum-safe
        communication, full-stack quantum computers, cryogenic components, precision lasers, atomic
        clocks and single-photon detectors. Selected startups can receive grants of up to about
        <strong>₹25 crore each</strong>, and a second cohort of up to 16 more was planned. The startup
        pipeline is run with the I-HUB Quantum Technology Foundation at IISER Pune.
      </p>
      <h3>Targets vs. delivery — an honest scorecard</h3>
      <p>
        Approved for the coming years, but not yet delivered: <strong>397 technology-development
        projects</strong> and a <strong>1,400-strong trained headcount in FY 2026-27</strong> (peaking at
        1,417 in 2027-28). Genuinely delivered so far: the four hubs are functional, the startup
        programme is running, and Indian teams have demonstrated real hardware milestones — including
        QpiAI's 25-qubit Indus system (April 2025) and multi-hundred-kilometre quantum key distribution.
        India's quantum research output itself has nearly tripled, from 222 papers in 2019 to 596 in
        2023. The mission's status was most recently reviewed in the Lok Sabha in February 2026.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <section class="keypoints">
    <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The Union Cabinet approved the National Quantum Mission on 19 April 2023: ₹6,003.65 crore over eight years (2023-24 to 2030-31), run by DST.</li>
      <li>Four verticals with staged targets: up to 1,000 physical qubits, 2,000-km satellite and fiber quantum communication, femto-Tesla sensing, and indigenous quantum materials.</li>
      <li>Four Thematic Hubs (IISc, IIT Madras + C-DOT, IIT Bombay, IIT Delhi) anchor 43 institutions and 152 researchers; fab facilities are coming up at four institutes.</li>
      <li>Eight startups were selected in November 2024 with grants up to ~₹25 crore each; the 2025-26 outlay of ₹1,261 crore is the mission's peak funding year.</li>
      <li>The honest caveat: only ₹32.77 crore of the ₹86 crore allocated in 2024-25 was utilized — spending, not sanctioning, is the mission's real test.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

